/*
In this implementation, we use an adjacency matrix to represent the graph. We initialize an array inDegree to store the indegrees
of each vertex by iterating over the adjacency matrix and adding up the number of incoming edges for each vertex.

We then add all the vertices with indegree 0 to a queue and perform a BFS-like traversal. For each dequeued vertex, we add it to
the sorted list and decrement the indegree of all its adjacent vertices. If the indegree of a vertex becomes 0, we add it to the 
queue.

If the sorted list has fewer vertices than the total number of vertices in the graph, it means the graph has a cycle, and we throw 
an IllegalStateException. Otherwise, we return the sorted list.

Topological sort is an algorithm used to order the vertices of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) such that for every 
directed edge (u, v), the vertex u comes before vertex v in the ordering.

In other words, it is a linear ordering of vertices such that if there is a directed edge from vertex u to vertex v, then u comes 
before v in the ordering. It is useful for tasks that have dependencies between them, where a task must be completed before 
another task can begin.

Topological sort is a graph algorithm that orders the vertices of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) such that for every directed 
edge (u, v), the vertex u comes before vertex v in the ordering. In other words, it is a linear ordering of vertices such that if 
there is a directed edge from vertex u to vertex v, then u comes before v in the ordering.

Topological sorting is useful for many applications, especially when we need to execute a set of tasks that have dependencies. 
For example, if we want to compile a program that consists of multiple source files, we need to compile the files in a specific 
order such that the files that depend on others are compiled later.

There are other algorithms to perform topological sorting, such as using Kahn's algorithm, which uses a queue to store 
vertices with indegree 0 and repeatedly removes them and their edges until all vertices are removed. 
*/
import java.util.*;

public class TSort 
{
    static int V; // number of vertices
    static int[][] adjMatrix; // adjacency matrix

    public TSort(int V) 
	{
        this.V = V;
        adjMatrix = new int[V][V];
    }

    public void addEdge(int u, int v) 
	{
        adjMatrix[u][v] = 1;
    }

    public List<Integer> sort() 
	{
        int[] inDegree = new int[V]; // array to store indegrees of vertices
        for (int u = 0; u < V; u++) 
		{
            for (int v = 0; v < V; v++) 
			{
                inDegree[v] += adjMatrix[u][v];
            }
        }

        Queue<Integer> queue = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int u = 0; u < V; u++)
		{
            if (inDegree[u] == 0) 
			{
                queue.offer(u);
            }
        }

        List<Integer> sortedList = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!queue.isEmpty())
		{
            int u = queue.poll();
            sortedList.add(u);
            for (int v = 0; v < V; v++) 
			{
                if (adjMatrix[u][v] != 0 && --inDegree[v] == 0) 
				{
                    queue.offer(v);
                }
            }
        }
        if (sortedList.size() != V) 
		{
            System.out.println("Graph has a cycle");
        }
        return sortedList;
    }
	public static void main(String[] args) 
	{
		Scanner s=new Scanner(System.in);
		V=s.nextInt();
		TSort g = new TSort(V);
		int E=s.nextInt();
		for(int i=1;i<=E;i++)
		{
			int u=s.nextInt();
			int v=s.nextInt();
			g.addEdge(u,v);
		}
        System.out.println("Topological Sort:");
        System.out.println(g.sort());
	}
}

