/*
397. Integer Replacement
Given a positive integer n, you can apply one of the following operations:
If n is even, replace n with n / 2.
If n is odd, replace n with either n + 1 or n - 1.
Return the minimum number of operations needed for n to become 1.

Example 1:
Input: 8
Output: 3
Explanation: 8 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1

Example 2:
Input: 7
Output: 4
Explanation: 7 -> 8 -> 4 -> 2 -> 1
or 7 -> 6 -> 3 -> 2 -> 1

Example 3:
Input: n = 4
Output: 2

Approach
Convert n to a long (a) to prevent overflow when n == Integer.MAX_VALUE.
Use a loop to reduce a to 1.
If a is even: divide it by 2.
If a is odd:
If a == 3 or (a - 1) % 4 == 0, subtract 1 (because 3 -> 2 -> 1 is optimal).
Otherwise, add 1 (since adding makes more trailing 0s in binary, which speeds up 
division).
Increment the operation count each time.
*/
import java.util.*;
class  IntRep
{
	public static int integerReplacement(int n) {
        long a =n;
        int count = 0;
        while(a!=1)
		{
            if(a%2==0)
			{
                a= a/2;
            }
            else
			{
                if(a==3 || (a-1)%4 == 0)
				{
                    a = a-1;
                }
				else
				{
                    a = a+1;
                }
            }
            ++count;
        }
        return count;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args)
	{
        Scanner s=new Scanner(System.in);
		int n=s.nextInt();
        System.out.println(integerReplacement(n));
    }
}
