/*
Problem: Minimum Cable Length to Turn Systems ON
The computer systems of N employees of a company are arranged in a row.
A technical fault in the power supply has caused some of the systems to turn OFF while the others
remain ON.
The employees whose systems are OFF are unable to work. The company does not like to see its 
employees sitting idle. So until the technical team can find the actual cause of the breakdown, the
technical head has devised a temporary workaround for the OFF systems using a minimum cost. 
They decide to connect all the OFF systems to the nearest ON system with the shortest possible
length of cable.

To make this happen, they calculate the distance of each system from the first system.

Write an algorithm to help the technical head find the minimum length of cable they need to turn 
all the systems ON.

Input:
The first line contains an integer systemState_size, representing the number of systems (N).

The second line contains N space-separated integers systemState[1], systemState[2], ...,
systemState[N], where each value is either 1 (ON) or 0 (OFF).

The third line contains an integer dist_size, representing the number of distances (M = N).

The fourth line contains M space-separated integers dist[1], dist[2], ..., dist[M],
where each value represents the distance of each system from the first system.

Output:
Print a single integer representing the minimum length of cable that the technical head needs 
to turn all systems ON. If no cable is needed, print 0.

Constraints:
1 <= systemState_size, dist_size <= 10^5
1 <= dist[i] < dist[i+1] <= 10^9

Example Input:
5
1 0 1 0 0
5
0 3 7 8 10
Example Output:
7

Explanation:
System at index 1 (distance 3) -> nearest ON system at index 0 (distance 0) -> cable = 3

System at index 3 (distance 8) -> nearest ON system at index 2 (distance 7) -> cable = 1

System at index 4 (distance 10) -> nearest ON system at index 2 (distance 7) -> cable = 3
Total = 3 + 1 + 3 = 7
*/
import java.util.*;

public class MinCableLength 
{

    public static int minLength(int[] systemState, int[] dist) 
	{
        List<Integer> onPositions = new ArrayList<>();
        int totalCableLength = 0;

        // Collect the positions (indices) of systems that are ON
        for (int i = 0; i < systemState.length; i++) 
		{
            if (systemState[i] == 1) 
			{
                onPositions.add(i);
            }
        }

        // If no ON system, OFF systems cannot be connected
        if (onPositions.size() == 0) 
			return 0;

        // For each OFF system, find nearest ON system and add cable length
        for (int i = 0; i < systemState.length; i++) 
		{
            if (systemState[i] == 0) 
			{
                int minDist = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
                for (int onIndex : onPositions) 
				{
                    int cable = Math.abs(dist[i] - dist[onIndex]);
                    minDist = Math.min(minDist, cable);
                }
                totalCableLength += minDist;
            }
        }

        return totalCableLength;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) 
	{
        Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);

        // Input for system state
        int systemState_size = in.nextInt();
        int[] systemState = new int[systemState_size];
        for (int i = 0; i < systemState_size; i++) 
		{
            systemState[i] = in.nextInt();
        }

        // Input for distances
        int dist_size = in.nextInt();
        int[] dist = new int[dist_size];
        for (int i = 0; i < dist_size; i++) 
		{
            dist[i] = in.nextInt();
        }

        // Output the result
        int result = minLength(systemState, dist);
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}

