/*
The Josephus Problem is a famous theoretical problem related to a certain elimination game:

Problem Statement
There are N people (numbered from 1 to N) standing in a circle. Every K-th person is eliminated until only one person remains. The objective is to determine the position of the survivor.

Example
Lets take an example with N = 7 people and K = 3:

People: 1 2 3 4 5 6 7

Eliminate the 3rd person ? Remaining: 1 2 4 5 6 7

Eliminate the 6th person ? Remaining: 1 2 4 5 7

Eliminate the 2nd person ? Remaining: 1 4 5 7

Eliminate the 7th person ? Remaining: 1 4 5

Eliminate the 5th person ? Remaining: 1 4

Eliminate the 1st person ? Survivor is 4

Recursive Formula
The Josephus position can be determined recursively:
J(n,k)=(J(n-1,k)+k)modn
Base case: When n = 1, the survivor is at position 0 (0-based index).

Recursive step: Reduce the problem to n - 1 people and adjust the survivors position.

Implementation
In the recursive function:

Start with n = 1, survivor at index 0.

Work backwards, adjusting the position using (J(n - 1, k) + k) % n.

Time Complexity
Recursive solution: 
O(N), as it makes N-1 recursive calls.

Iterative version: Can also be solved in 

O(N) time using an iterative approach.
*/

